**user**:
> In Julia, how do I get a column from a DataFrame given a symbol for that column?
**assistant**:
> In Julia, you can access a column in a `DataFrame` by using the column name as a symbol within square brackets. If `df` is your DataFrame and `:column_name` is the symbol for your column, you can retrieve the column like this:
> 
> ```julia
> column_data = df[:column_name]
> ```
> 
> This will give you the contents of the column as a `Vector`. This method is particularly useful if the column name has special characters or if you want to programmatically access columns by symbols.
**user**:
> How about if I also have a row number?
**assistant**:
> If you want to get a specific value from a DataFrame in Julia using both a column symbol and a row number, you can do so by indexing into the DataFrame with both row and column specifications. 
> 
> Here’s how you can do it:
> 
> ```julia
> value = df[row_number, :column_name]
> ```
> 
> In this example:
> 
> - `row_number` is the index of the row you want to access (make sure it is within the bounds of the DataFrame).
> - `:column_name` is the symbol representing the column you wish to access.
> 
> This will return the specific value located at the intersection of the specified row and column. 
> 
> For example, if you want to access the value in the first row and a column named `:age`, you would write:
> 
> ```julia
> value = df[1, :age]
> ``` 
> 
> Make sure you adjust `row_number` and `:column_name` to match your DataFrame's structure!
